---
import SimulatorLayout from '../../layouts/SimulatorLayout.astro';
import AdaptationsSim from '../../simulators/adaptations/AdaptationsSim.astro';
import { adaptationsQuiz } from '../../data/quizzes/adaptations';

const toc = [
  { id: 'what', label: 'What is an adaptation?' },
  { id: 'kinds', label: 'Structural, behavioural and physiological' },
  { id: 'heat', label: 'Staying warm and keeping cool' },
  { id: 'water', label: 'Saving water' },
  { id: 'food', label: 'Getting food' },
  { id: 'predators', label: 'Avoiding predators' },
  { id: 'evolution', label: 'How adaptations arise' },
  { id: 'terms', label: 'Key terms' },
  { id: 'misconceptions', label: 'Common misconceptions' },
  { id: 'takeaways', label: 'Key takeaways' },
];

const references = [
  { text: 'Schmidt-Nielsen, K. (1997). Animal Physiology: Adaptation and Environment (5th ed.). Cambridge University Press.' },
  { text: 'Clark, M. A., Douglas, M., & Choi, J. (2018). Biology 2e, Section 19.3: Adaptive Evolution. OpenStax.', url: 'https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/19-3-adaptive-evolution' },
  { text: 'Mayr, E. (1956). Geographical character gradients and climatic adaptation. Evolution, 10(1), 105–108.' },
  { text: 'Allen, J. A. (1877). The influence of physical conditions in the genesis of species. Radical Review, 1, 108–140.' },
];
---

<SimulatorLayout
  branch="zoology"
  topic="animal-adaptations"
  description="Interactive animal adaptations experiment: design an animal’s fur, size, ears, coat colour, diet and behaviour, then test how well it survives in the Arctic, desert, forest or grassland."
  toc={toc}
  quiz={adaptationsQuiz}
  references={references}
>
  <Fragment slot="intro">
    <p>
      Why does an Arctic fox look so different from a desert fox? Every animal’s body and behaviour are tuned to where it
      lives. Design your own animal, choose its fur, size, ears, coat colour, diet and habits, then drop it into a habitat
      and see whether it can keep warm or cool, find water and food, and hide from predators.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <AdaptationsSim slot="sim" />

  <Fragment slot="instructions">
    <ol>
      <li>Choose a <strong>habitat</strong>. You can also adjust its temperature, food supply and number of predators.</li>
      <li>Design <strong>your animal</strong>: fur, size, ears, coat colour, diet and behaviour.</li>
      <li>Read the four bars and the <strong>survival</strong> figure. Faded animals in the scene are those that would not survive the year.</li>
      <li>Follow the advice to fix the biggest problem, or press <strong>Show a well-adapted animal</strong> for one solution.</li>
      <li>Try moving a well-adapted animal to a different habitat. What goes wrong?</li>
    </ol>
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="what">What is an adaptation?</h2>
  <p>
    An <strong>adaptation</strong> is an inherited characteristic that helps an organism survive and reproduce in its
    environment. Adaptations are not changes an individual makes because it needs to; they are features passed on in
    genes that became common over many generations because individuals that had them left more offspring.
  </p>

  <h2 id="kinds">Structural, behavioural and physiological</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Structural</strong> adaptations are body features: thick fur, sharp claws, camouflage colours, webbed feet.</li>
    <li><strong>Behavioural</strong> adaptations are actions: hibernating, migrating, hunting in packs, being active at night.</li>
    <li><strong>Physiological</strong> adaptations are processes inside the body: producing very concentrated urine to save water, or antifreeze proteins in polar fish.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="heat">Staying warm and keeping cool</h2>
  <p>
    Mammals and birds keep a steady body temperature, so they must balance heat made against heat lost. In the cold,
    thick fur or feathers and layers of fat (blubber) insulate the body. Large, compact bodies lose heat more slowly because
    they have less surface area for their volume (<strong>Bergmann’s rule</strong>), and small ears, tails and limbs lose
    less heat (<strong>Allen’s rule</strong>). In the heat the reverse helps: thin fur, slim bodies and big ears that work
    like radiators, as in the fennec fox and African elephant.
  </p>

  <h2 id="water">Saving water</h2>
  <p>
    Desert animals survive with little or no drinking water. Many are small and spend the hot day in cool, humid burrows,
    coming out at night. Kangaroo rats make extremely concentrated urine and get almost all their water from their food.
    Camels can lose a quarter of their body water and refill quickly, and let their body temperature rise during the day
    instead of sweating.
  </p>

  <h2 id="food">Getting food</h2>
  <p>
    Teeth, beaks, claws and digestive systems match an animal’s diet: sharp teeth for meat, broad grinding teeth for
    plants, long sticky tongues for insects. Where food disappears in winter, animals may <strong>hibernate</strong>
    (dormice, bears), <strong>migrate</strong> (many birds) or store food (squirrels).
  </p>

  <h2 id="predators">Avoiding predators</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Camouflage</strong> makes animals hard to spot against their background: snowy white in the Arctic, sandy in
    the desert, dappled browns in the forest. Some animals change colour with the seasons, such as the Arctic hare. Others
    rely on speed, size, armour, living in groups, warning colours or being active when predators are not.
  </p>

  <h2 id="evolution">How adaptations arise</h2>
  <p>
    Individuals in a population vary. Those whose features suit the environment are more likely to survive and pass on
    their genes, so over many generations helpful features become common: this is <strong>natural selection</strong>.
    Adaptations are always a compromise and only help in a particular environment, which is why rapid change such as
    climate warming or new predators can leave well-adapted species struggling.
  </p>

  <h2 id="terms">Key terms</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt>Camouflage</dt>
    <dd>Colours or patterns that help an animal blend into its surroundings.</dd>
    <dt>Nocturnal</dt>
    <dd>Active at night and resting during the day.</dd>
    <dt>Surface area to volume ratio</dt>
    <dd>How much outside surface an animal has compared with its body volume; it controls how fast heat is lost.</dd>
  </dl>

  <h2 id="misconceptions">Common misconceptions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>“Animals adapt because they want or need to.”</strong> Individuals cannot change their genes; populations change over generations.</li>
    <li><strong>“There is one perfect animal.”</strong> A feature that helps in one place can be a handicap in another.</li>
    <li><strong>“Adaptations are always physical.”</strong> Behaviour and body chemistry matter just as much.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="takeaways">Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Adaptations are inherited features that help survival and reproduction in a particular environment.</li>
    <li>They can be structural, behavioural or physiological.</li>
    <li>Body size, fur and ear size control heat balance; camouflage, diet and behaviour solve other challenges.</li>
    <li>Natural selection makes useful adaptations common over many generations.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="muted">Model note: survival scores are simplified rules based on real patterns (Bergmann’s and Allen’s rules, camouflage, water balance), not measurements of real species.</p>
</SimulatorLayout>
